Below is a **step-by-step recipe** that turns a set of plain-green / plain-brown point icons into symbols that stay readable on any satellite / aerial background (crop fields, forest canopy, bare soil, rooftops, water, snow, etc.).  
The recipe follows the cartographic principles in the **maplibre-cartography** skill:

| Problem | Why the usual approach fails | What you need to add |
|---------|-----------------------------|----------------------|
| **Camouflage** – a green icon blends with a green park, a brown icon with dry earth | The raster imagery has no single “background colour” you can match. The icon’s hue is the only thing that separates it from the photo, and most natural colours are already present in the scene. | **Add a contrasting halo (or outline) that is independent of the icon’s fill colour**. Use a **saturated, earth-tone fill** for the icon itself, then a **dark semi-transparent halo** (or a light halo on a dark background) that is drawn by MapLibre, not baked into the PNG. |
| **Flat-look** – the icon looks like a sticker that floats above the photo | A flat colour with no shading gives no figure-ground cue. | **Add a contact-shadow / subtle drop-shadow** that is anchored to the ground, and optionally a slight gradient on the icon so it looks three-dimensional. |
| **Layer ordering** – the icon ends up under basemap labels or over other data you don’t want to hide | MapLibre draws layers in the order they appear in the style. | Insert your symbol layer **just before the first basemap `symbol` layer** (or after your own data layers but before the basemap’s label layers). |
| **Accessibility** – low contrast for colour-blind users | A dark halo on a bright background is the most WCAG-friendly combination. | Use a **dark, semi-transparent halo** (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) and a **bright, saturated fill** (`#ffb400` amber, `#d84315` terracotta, etc.). |

Below you’ll find the **complete workflow** – from preparing the SVG, turning it into an SDF sprite, adding it to the map, and styling the layer – plus a few optional extras (contact-shadow, data-driven colour switches) if you want even more robustness.

---

## 1️⃣ Prepare the SVG as an SDF sprite

### Why SDF?
* Only SDF (Signed Distance Field) images can be given a **halo** (`icon-halo-color`, `icon-halo-width`) and a **color tint** (`icon-color`) at render time.  
* A regular PNG or raster SVG will not respond to those paint properties.

### Minimal SVG template

```svg
<svg viewBox="0 0 64 64" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
  <!-- Transparent padding (12 px) so the halo isn’t clipped -->
  <g transform="translate(12,12)">
    <!-- The shape you want to colour – use a solid fill (any colour, it will be overridden) -->
    <path d="M20,0 L44,0 L44,44 L20,44 Z" fill="#ff0000"/>
    <!-- Optional inner gradient – keep it inside the shape; it will be preserved when the image is rendered as SDF -->
    <defs>
      <linearGradient id="grad" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="0%" y2="100%">
        <stop offset="0%" stop-color="#fff" stop-opacity="0.8"/>
        <stop offset="100%" stop-color="#ccc" stop-opacity="0.6"/>
      </linearGradient>
    </defs>
    <path d="M20,0 L44,0 L44,44 L20,44 Z" fill="url(#grad)"/>
  </g>
</svg>
```

* **Padding** (`translate(12,12)`) guarantees the halo (and any shadow you bake in) has room to expand.  
* Keep the **viewBox** square (or the aspect ratio you need) – MapLibre will scale it with `icon-size`.  
* **Do not** embed a raster drop-shadow; we’ll add a *contact-shadow* with CSS-like filters later.

### Convert to an SDF sprite at runtime

```js
// Helper: fetch the SVG, rasterise it, then add as an SDF image
async function addSdfIcon(map, id, url) {
  const resp = await fetch(url);
  const svgText = await resp.text();

  // Create an Image element from the SVG string
  const img = new Image();
  const svgBlob = new Blob([svgText], {type: 'image/svg+xml'});
  img.src = URL.createObjectURL(svgBlob);

  await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    img.onload = () => resolve();
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(e);
  });

  // Add as an SDF image – the `sdf: true` flag tells MapLibre to treat the alpha channel as a distance field
  map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: true, pixelRatio: 2 }); // pixelRatio matches your device / sprite resolution
}
```

> **Tip:** If you have many icons, pre-build a sprite sheet (see the `maplibre-sprites-icons` skill) and host it as `sprite.json` + `sprite.png`. The same `sdf: true` flag is set in the JSON for each SDF entry.

---

## 2️⃣ Add the icon layer with a dark halo

```js
// 1️⃣ Load the SDF icon (run once after the map is ready)
addSdfIcon(map, 'poi-amber', '/icons/poi-amber.svg');

// 2️⃣ Add a source for your POI data (GeoJSON example)
map.addSource('my-pois', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/pois.geojson'
});

// 3️⃣ Insert the symbol layer *just before* the first basemap label layer
const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id || null;

map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-poi-symbols',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'my-pois',
  layout: {
    // Use the SDF icon we added
    'icon-image': 'poi-amber',
    // Scale with zoom – 0.8 at z10 → 1.4 at z16 (adjust to your needs)
    'icon-size': [
      'interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'],
      10, 0.8,
      16, 1.4
    ],
    // Allow many points to overlap; the halo will keep them readable
    'icon-allow-overlap': true,
    'icon-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    // 1️⃣ Saturated fill – we colour-tint the SDF shape
    'icon-color': '#ffb400',               // amber – high chroma, works on both green & brown backgrounds
    // 2️⃣ Dark semi-transparent halo (the “outline” that works on any imagery)
    'icon-halo-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.75)',
    'icon-halo-width': 2,                  // pixels; 1–2 works well for 12-24 px icons
    // 3️⃣ Optional subtle contact-shadow (requires the icon to be SDF)
    //    The shadow is a second halo that is offset down-right and blurred.
    //    MapLibre does not have a built-in shadow, so we fake it with a second halo
    //    using a data-driven expression that adds a tiny extra halo only on the bottom side.
    //    (If you prefer a baked-in shadow, add a blurred ellipse under the shape in the SVG.)
    //    Here we just keep the halo thin; you can increase width for a “sticker” look.
    // 4️⃣ Keep the icon on top of your own polygon/line data but under basemap labels
    //    (the insertion point above already guarantees that)
  }
}, firstSymbolId);   // <-- insert before the first basemap symbol layer
```

### What each paint property does

| Paint property | Effect on figure-ground |
|----------------|--------------------------|
| **`icon-color`** | Tints the SDF shape. Choose a **high-chroma, earth-tone** (amber, terracotta, teal) that is *not* common in the imagery. These hues stand out because most aerial photos are desaturated. |
| **`icon-halo-color`** | The halo is drawn *outside* the icon’s alpha edge, so it never blends with the background. A **dark, semi-transparent** halo (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) works on bright crops, dark forests, water, snow, etc. |
| **`icon-halo-width`** | 1–2 px is enough for a 12-24 px icon. Wider halos increase legibility but add visual weight; keep it thin unless the icon must dominate the map. |
| **`icon-allow-overlap`** | Prevents MapLibre from hiding overlapping icons; the halo still protects each label from the background. |
| **`icon-ignore-placement`** (optional) | If you have a very dense POI layer and you *must* show every point, set this to `true`. The halo will keep each point readable, but you may get visual clutter. |

> **Accessibility note:** The combination of a bright fill (`#ffb400`) and a dark halo (`rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`) yields a **contrast ratio > 7:1** for the *combined* icon+halo against any background, satisfying WCAG AA for large text/graphics.

---

## 3️⃣ Optional: Add a baked-in contact-shadow (extra grounding)

If you want the icon to *feel* anchored to the ground (especially for larger symbols like airport markers), add a faint ellipse underneath the shape in the SVG:

```svg
<g transform="translate(12,12)">
  <!-- Contact shadow – a blurred dark ellipse -->
  <ellipse cx="32" cy="48" rx="20" ry="6"
           fill="rgba(0,0,0,0.35)" filter="url(#blur)"/>
  <defs>
    <filter id="blur">
      <feGaussianBlur stdDeviation="3"/>
    </filter>
  </defs>

  <!-- The actual icon (same as before) -->
  <path d="M20,0 L44,0 L44,44 L20,44 Z" fill="#ffb400"/>
</g>
```

* Keep the **shadow inside the padded area** (the 12 px padding) so it isn’t clipped.  
* The shadow is baked into the sprite, so it works even for non-SDF icons, but you lose the ability to change its colour dynamically.

---

## 4️⃣ Layer ordering checklist (so your icons never hide basemap labels)

```js
// 1️⃣ Background (already in the style)
// 2️⃣ Raster imagery (satellite)
// 3️⃣ Hillshade (if any)
// 4️⃣ Terrain fills (land, water, parks)
// 5️⃣ Basemap line layers (roads, boundaries)
// 6️⃣ **Your own polygon/line data** (if any)
// 7️⃣ **Your point-symbol layer** (inserted *before* the first basemap symbol)
// 8️⃣ Basemap symbol layers (place names, road labels)
// 9️⃣ **Your own label layers** (if you need extra POI text – add after step 8)
```

If you ever add a new data source at runtime, use the same insertion pattern:

```js
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
map.addLayer(yourLayerDefinition, firstSymbol);
```

---

## 5️⃣ Quick “what-if” variations

| Situation | Recommended tweak |
|-----------|-------------------|
| **Your icons must be *very* small (≤ 10 px)** | Reduce `icon-halo-width` to **1 px** and increase `icon-halo-opacity` (`rgba(0,0,0,0.85)`). A thin, strong halo is more effective at tiny sizes. |
| **Your map is a dark-tone satellite (e.g., night-time imagery)** | Switch the halo to a **light** semi-transparent halo (`rgba(255,255,255,0.75)`) and keep the fill bright. |
| **You need two colour families (e.g., “park” vs. “historic”)** | Keep the same dark halo for both, but vary `icon-color` (`#4caf50` for parks, `#c2185b` for historic). The halo guarantees both stay legible. |
