Below is a **step-by-step recipe** for “fly-to” navigation that feels smooth, stays on-brand with cartographic best-practice, and works on any MapLibre GL JS map (v2.x or later).

---

## 1. What “fly” really means in MapLibre

| API | What it does | When to use it |
|-----|--------------|----------------|
| `map.flyTo(options)` | Animates the **camera** (center, zoom, bearing, pitch) with a *smooth, curved* trajectory that mimics a real-world flight. | Most UI-driven jumps – button clicks, menu selections, “go to my location”, etc. |
| `map.easeTo(options)` | Same as `flyTo` but follows a **straight-line** interpolation (no “overshoot”). | When you want a quick, subtle pan/zoom without the “flight” feel. |
| `map.jumpTo(options)` | Instantly snaps the camera – no animation. | For initial load or when you must avoid any motion (e.g., screen-reader mode). |

**Tip:** `flyTo` automatically calculates an easing curve that keeps the motion fluid even when the distance is large. You only need to supply the target camera values (and optionally tweak duration/easing).

---

## 2. Minimal HTML & CSS

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre flyTo demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    .control {
      position: absolute;
      top: 10px; left: 10px;
      background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
      padding: 6px 10px;
      border-radius: 4px;
      font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;
      cursor: pointer;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div class="control" id="flyBtn">✈️ Fly to Paris</div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="fly.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

*The button (`#flyBtn`) is deliberately given a semi-transparent white background so it stays readable on any basemap (including satellite imagery).*

---

## 3. JavaScript – the “fly” logic (`fly.js`)

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style – vector, raster, or imagery)
// ---------------------------------------------------
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // change to your own style URL
  center: [-0.1276, 51.5074], // London
  zoom: 10,
  pitch: 0,
  bearing: 0,
  // If you are using satellite imagery, make sure you have a dark halo for labels
  // (see the cartography skill for the exact halo settings).
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣  Helper: build a reusable fly-to function
// ---------------------------------------------------
/**
 * Fly the camera to a target location.
 *
 * @param {Array<number>} lngLat   – [lon, lat] in EPSG:4326
 * @param {number}        zoom     – Desired zoom level (optional)
 * @param {object}        opts     – Additional MapLibre flyTo options
 */
function flyToLocation(lngLat, zoom = 12, opts = {}) {
  // Default animation parameters – feel free to tweak
  const defaults = {
    center: lngLat,
    zoom,
    // Keep the current bearing/pitch unless you explicitly change them
    bearing: map.getBearing(),
    pitch: map.getPitch(),
    // Duration in ms – 1000 ms is a good baseline for medium distances
    duration: 1500,
    // The built-in “fly” easing (cubic-bezier) works well; you can replace it
    // with a custom easing function if you need a different feel.
    // easing: t => t, // linear (not recommended)
  };

  map.flyTo(Object.assign({}, defaults, opts));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 3️⃣  Wire the button click
// ---------------------------------------------------
document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  // Example target: Paris, France
  const paris = [2.3522, 48.8566];

  // Optional: add a slight tilt so the user perceives depth
  const extraOpts = {
    pitch: 45,          // tilt the camera
    bearing: 30,        // rotate a bit for visual interest
    // You can also add a `essential: true` flag for reduced-motion users
    // (see the Accessibility section below)
  };

  flyToLocation(paris, 13, extraOpts);
});

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 4️⃣  Accessibility – respect reduced-motion preferences
// ---------------------------------------------------
if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
  // Override the default flyTo with an instant jump for users who
  // have asked for reduced motion.
  document.getElementById('flyBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
    map.jumpTo({ center: [2.3522, 48.8566], zoom: 13, pitch: 0, bearing: 0 });
  });
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Init** | Creates a MapLibre map. The style can be any vector or raster basemap. If you use satellite imagery, remember the halo recommendation from the cartography skill (`text-halo-color: rgba(0,0,0,0.75)`). |
| **flyToLocation** | A tiny wrapper that supplies sane defaults (`duration: 1500 ms`, keeps current bearing/pitch) and lets you override any of them. |
| **Button handler** | Calls the wrapper with Paris coordinates, a zoom of 13, and a modest pitch/bearing to give a “3-D-fly” feel. |
| **Reduced-motion** | Detects the user’s OS setting (`prefers-reduced-motion`) and falls back to `jumpTo` (instant) to avoid unwanted animation. This satisfies WCAG 2.1 AA for motion-sensitive users. |

---

## 4. Fine-tuning the animation

| Parameter | Typical values | When to adjust |
|-----------|----------------|----------------|
| `duration` | 800 – 2500 ms (shorter for nearby points, longer for continent-scale jumps) | Large geographic jumps → increase; UI-driven quick hops → decrease. |
| `curve` (internal) | Default is a cubic-bezier that gives a “flight-arc”. You can supply a custom easing function (`easing: t => t*t`) if you want a slower start and faster finish. |
| `maxDuration` | 20000 ms (max cap) | Prevents extremely long flights when the distance is huge. |
| `offset` | `[x, y]` in pixels | Useful when you want the target point to sit slightly off-center (e.g., to keep a UI panel visible). |
| `essential` | `true` | Marks the animation as essential for the app’s functionality; browsers may ignore it for reduced-motion users. Use sparingly. |

**Example with a custom easing curve:**

```js
flyToLocation(paris, 13, {
  duration: 2000,
  // Ease-in-out cubic (same as CSS `cubic-bezier(0.25,0.1,0.25,1)`)
  easing: t => (--t) * t * t + 1
});
```

---

## 5. Keeping your labels readable during the flight

When the camera tilts (`pitch > 0`) or rotates (`bearing !== 0`), MapLibre automatically re-projects symbols so they stay upright. However, on **satellite imagery** you must ensure the label halo is dark enough to stay legible throughout the animation.

```json
{
  "type": "symbol",
  "layout": { "text-field": "{name}" },
  "paint": {
    "text-color": "#ffffff",
    "text-halo-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.75)",
    "text-halo-width": 1.2
  }
}
```

If you have custom POI icons, give them a thin halo (`icon-halo-width: 1`) and a dark halo color (`icon-halo-color: "rgba(0,0,0,0.6)"`) so they stay visible against bright roofs or snow.

---

## 6. Full-screen example (run it locally)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>MapLibre flyTo demo</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <style>
    body,html{margin:0;height:100%}
    #map{position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%}
    .btn{
      position:absolute;top:12px;left:12px;
      background:rgba(255,255,255,0.9);padding:6px 10px;
      border-radius:4px;cursor:pointer;font-family:sans-serif;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<div class="btn" id="flyBtn">✈️ Fly to Tokyo</div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco
    zoom: 10
  });

  function flyToLocation(lngLat, zoom = 12, opts = {}) {
    const defaults = {
      center: lngLat,
      zoom,
      bearing: map.getBearing(),
      pitch: map.getPitch(),
      duration: 1500
    };
    map.flyTo(Object.assign({}, defaults, opts));
  }

  const btn = document.getElementById('flyBtn');
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const tokyo = [139.6917, 35.6895];
    flyToLocation(tokyo, 13, { pitch: 45, bearing: -30 });
  });

  // Reduced-motion fallback
  if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      map.jumpTo({ center: [139.6917, 35.6895], zoom: 13 });
    });
  }
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the file in a browser, click the button, and watch the smooth flight from San Francisco to Tokyo.

---

## 7. Checklist – Did you cover everything?

- [ ] **Camera parameters** (`center`, `zoom`, `bearing`, `pitch`) are set correctly for the target.
- [ ] **Duration & easing** feel natural for the distance (test at several zoom levels).
- [ ] **Label halos** follow the cartographic guidance (dark semi-transparent halo on imagery, light halo on light vector basemaps).
- [ ] **Icon halos** are added for any SDF sprites you use on imagery.
- [ ] **Reduced-motion** preference is respected (`prefers-reduced-motion` → `jumpTo` or `flyTo(..., { essential: true })`).
- [ ] **Layer order**: your data layers are inserted **before** the first `symbol` layer so they never cover basemap labels (see the “injection pattern” in the cartography skill).
- [ ] **Accessibility**: text-halo contrast meets WCAG 2.1 AA (≥ 4.5:1 for normal text).  

If any of those items are missing, adjust the style or JavaScript accordingly.

